{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Stand firm and see.</b> Perhaps it intends to tell them that they should stand firm in prayer, just as they had been standing and crying out to HaShem as stated nearby: ״וַיִּצְעֲקוּ וְגוֹ׳ אֶל ה׳״, and likewise it says (I Samuel 1:26): ״אֲנִי הָאִשָּׁה הַנִּצֶּבֶת עִמְּכָה״ etc." "<b>Which HaShem will do for you today.</b> In saying <b>today</b> He intended to remove from their hearts the misgiving concerning what had occurred during the Exodus from Egypt — that the end of redemption was prolonged from the day the tidings began until the completion of the matter, twelve months. Therefore he told them that the time of this war would not be prolonged, but rather today HaShem would accomplish the salvation." "<b>Whom you have seen, the Egyptians</b> etc. — the intention of this statement is perhaps that He sought to remove their fear in this manner, by saying: Is it not the case that your seeing the Egyptians today is for the reason that you will not see them again forever. For this reason HaShem brought them and HaShem showed them to you, in order that no trace of them should remain for you forever. And it is the preceding statement that He said to them (Exodus 14:4): ״וְחִזַּקְתִּי אֶת לֵב פַּרְעֹה״ etc. And although they saw the minister [of Egypt], him too will be measured out before them, as indeed came to pass."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:52.936569877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}